LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The sourcing problem & the two documents", "13", "<p>Procurement 2 stored AGREED rates; Procurement 3 assembled DEMAND. Between demand and commitment sits a question this module owns: <b>who should we buy this from, at what terms — and how do we know?</b> The answer is competitive sourcing, run through two documents.</p><p><b>The pair.</b> The <b>Request for Quotation (RFQ)</b> is the ASK: one document naming the items, quantities and required dates, addressed to a chosen set of suppliers. The <b>Supplier Quotation</b> is the ANSWER: each supplier's offer captured as its own document — rates, lead times, validity, terms — comparable side by side because they answer the same ask. Ask once, answer many, compare, decide, award: the whole module in one sentence.</p><p><b>When to source competitively — the four triggers.</b> Not every purchase is a competition; agreed rates exist precisely so routine buying skips the ceremony (the supplier-scoped Item Prices of Procurement 2 ARE past sourcing decisions, stored). The RFQ machinery fires on four occasions:</p><ul><li><b>New item or new category</b> — no agreed rate exists; the market must be asked before the first commitment (Procurement 3's chiller request is exactly this case, and Chapter 8 runs it).</li><li><b>Volume tier change</b> — what was bought in tens is about to be bought in hundreds; yesterday's rate was priced for yesterday's volume, and the new tier deserves fresh competition.</li><li><b>The periodic market test</b> — incumbent rates, however comfortable, are re-quoted on a calendar (annually for major categories is a common posture), because an agreed rate never tested against the market slowly becomes a loyalty tax (Chapter 7 owns the cadence).</li><li><b>The concentration remedy</b> — Procurement 1's tree flagged a category owned by one supplier; the RFQ is how a second source is found and priced before the single point of failure fails.</li></ul><p><b>What sourcing produces, beyond a price.</b> Run properly, the cycle leaves a <b>decision record</b>: who was asked, who answered what, why the winner won — filed against the documents. That record is the audit's answer, the next negotiation's baseline, and the institutional memory that survives the buyer's resignation. Run improperly — quotes gathered by phone, compared in someone's head, decided over lunch — the same purchase happens with none of those assets, and Chapter 9 meets what that costs. The module's thesis, stated now and proven by Chapter 9: <b>sourcing is a decision record, not a lunch.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The RFQ, field by field", "13", "<p>The ask determines the quality of every answer. A sloppy RFQ produces incomparable quotes and a comparison meeting full of apologies; a precise one produces answers that line up like columns. This chapter is the precision.</p><ul><li><b>The suppliers list.</b> One RFQ addresses MANY suppliers — the competition lives inside one document, which is what makes the answers comparable and the record whole. Choosing the list is half the sourcing decision: qualified candidates (Procurement 1's onboarding gate — an RFQ to an unvetted supplier is an invitation you may regret winning), the incumbent where one exists (they compete, not coast), and enough entrants for real competition (three is the customary floor for meaningful purchases — Chapter 7's policy owns the thresholds).</li><li><b>The items grid.</b> Item, quantity, UOM, and required-by date per row — inherited from the Material Request where sourcing began there (the create-from spine: RFQ made FROM the request keeps the demand ancestry visible). Precision points: quantities are the REAL intended volumes (a quote for 50 cartons priced against an ask of 500 is worthless — suppliers price to volume, so the ask must tell the truth); UOMs are explicit (Carton-12, not <i>cartons</i> — the comparison of Chapter 4 dies on ambiguous units); and specifications ride the row or its attachments, complete enough that answers are for the SAME thing.</li><li><b>The message and terms of the ask.</b> The RFQ carries your message to suppliers: the deadline for responses, the validity you need on their offers (long enough to decide and award), delivery expectations, and any commercial conditions (payment-terms expectations, delivery point). Stating the decision timeline is both courtesy and craft — suppliers price sharper for asks that will actually decide.</li><li><b>Sending.</b> The RFQ goes out from the system — emailed to each supplier's contact (the primary-flag hygiene of Procurement 1 earning rent: the ask must land in a live inbox), with portal-response links where the supplier portal is in use, letting suppliers key their own quotations directly. Either way, the send is on the record: who was asked, when, is a lookup forever.</li></ul><p><b>One ask, whole and honest.</b> The anti-patterns to refuse at this stage: the serial RFQ (asking suppliers one at a time as answers disappoint — the record fragments and the timeline lies), the vague ask (letting each supplier assume their own quantities and specs — guaranteeing apples against oranges), and the fake ask (RFQs sent with the winner pre-decided, to manufacture a paper trail — Chapter 9 convicts the three-quote theatre in full). The ask's integrity is the comparison's integrity, three chapters early.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Supplier Quotations: capturing the answers", "13", "<p>Answers arrive as PDFs, emails, portal entries, and phone calls. The discipline of this chapter is converting all of them into <b>Supplier Quotation documents</b> — one per supplier, against the RFQ — so the comparison compares records, not recollections.</p><p><b>What the quotation captures.</b> Per supplier: the rates per item row, in THEIR stated currency and UOM; their <b>lead time</b> (as stated for this volume, which may differ from the master's routine figure); the <b>validity</b> of their offer (the window you can award within); their payment-terms position; minimum order quantities and pack constraints; and any conditions riding the offer (delivery point, partial-shipment stance). Entered by procurement from their document, or keyed by the supplier through the portal — either way it becomes a record linked to the RFQ, and the paper original files against it.</p><p><b>Normalisation — the craft that makes comparison possible.</b> Suppliers answer in their own vocabularies, and raw answers do not line up: one quotes per Carton-12, another per unit; one in NGN, another in USD; one delivered-to-you, another ex-their-warehouse. The normalisation pass converts every answer to a <b>common basis</b> before any comparison: one UOM (the stock unit is the honest denominator — Buying 2's conversion arithmetic), one currency (converted at a stated rate, the same rate for all), one delivery basis (the ex-warehouse quote gets your haulage added; the import quote gets the FULL landed stack — Procurement 2, Chapter 7's thinking, applied here, because a $-per-unit factory price compared raw against a naira delivered price is the single most common way sourcing decisions go wrong). The normalised figures live on the comparison; the original figures stay on the quotations; both are kept, because the audit trail must show the arithmetic.</p><p><b>Incomplete and non-conforming answers.</b> The quote that skips two items, changes the quantities, or substitutes a <i>similar</i> product is captured AS GIVEN and flagged — never silently completed by assumption. Follow-up is legitimate (a clarifying question, a request to quote the missing lines, with the deadline held); assumption is not, because the figure you assumed becomes the figure you awarded, and the dispute arrives with the goods.</p><p><b>Validity discipline.</b> Every captured quote carries its expiry, and the sourcing timeline respects it: a decision made against expired offers is a decision made against numbers nobody is bound by. Where deciding takes longer than validity allows, extensions are requested explicitly — a one-line confirmation, on the record — not presumed.</p><p><b>The quiet asset.</b> Even LOSING quotations are market data: this supplier's pricing posture, that one's lead-time honesty, the market's range for the category. Filed against the RFQ, they price the next negotiation before it starts.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The comparison discipline", "14", "<p>All answers in, normalised, valid: now the comparison — and the discipline that keeps it from collapsing into <i>cheapest row wins</i>.</p><p><b>The comparison view.</b> Quotations against one RFQ line up side by side, per item row: each supplier's (normalised) rate, the totals, and the captured non-price facts. The system's comparison view does the arraying; the professional does the reading — and reads MORE than the price column.</p><p><b>The dimensions beyond price, each with its arithmetic:</b></p><ul><li><b>Lead time</b> — priced by what stock it forces you to carry: the supplier 8% cheaper with double the lead time obliges deeper safety stock and earlier cash commitment (Buying 2, Chapter 5's arithmetic); the true gap is the price gap MINUS the carrying difference, and sometimes it inverts.</li><li><b>Payment terms</b> — Net-30 against advance-payment is working capital with a value: the advance-only quote ties your cash for the lead time; the terms quote is the supplier funding you (Procurement 1, Chapter 4's credit-line frame). Comparable quotes are compared at comparable terms, or the difference is priced in.</li><li><b>MOQ and pack constraints</b> — the low rate at a minimum of 500 cartons is a different offer from the fair rate at 100: the excess is warehousing, cash, and expiry risk (for dated goods, decisive).</li><li><b>Validity and rate stability</b> — the offer locked six months against the offer <i>subject to review</i>: stability has value in a moving-cost economy, and the locked offer is buying you Procurement 2's validity-dated certainty.</li><li><b>The supplier facts</b> — reliability history where an incumbent competes (the scorecard thread Procurement 8 completes), compliance standing for regulated goods (the NAFDAC gate — a cheap quote from an unverifiable source is not a quote, it is a liability with a price tag), and concentration effects (awarding yet another category to the dominant supplier deepens the single point of failure the sourcing was meant to remedy).</li></ul><p><b>Landed comparison for the import lane.</b> The Mumbai quote competes against Lagos quotes ONLY in landed terms: the full stack (freight, duty, clearing at honest FX — Procurement 2, Chapter 7) plus the lead-time and cash-cycle arithmetic above. The comparison sheet shows the stack, so the decision inherits its assumptions visibly — including the break-even FX rate at which the import advantage dies.</p><p><b>Scoring, kept honest.</b> For consequential sourcing, a simple weighted read (price, lead, terms, risk — weights declared BEFORE answers open) disciplines the decision and documents it. The dishonest version — weights adjusted after the fact until the preferred supplier wins — is worse than no scoring, because it dresses a lunch as a method. Declare, then score; where judgment overrides the score, the override is written down with its reason, which is legitimate and auditable in a way retro-fitted weights never are.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Deciding well & the award", "13", "<p>The comparison read; now the decision — and the craft of awarding in a way that leaves records, relationships, and rates all in better condition than you found them.</p><p><b>The decision note.</b> One short paragraph, filed with the comparison: who won, and WHY — the price-and-beyond reasoning, the score if one ran, any judgment override with its reason. Sixty seconds of writing that becomes: the audit's answer, the baseline for the next sourcing of this category, and the institutional memory that outlives the buyer. The decision nobody wrote down is the decision the firm makes again from scratch next year — often differently, for no reason anyone can name.</p><p><b>The award mechanics.</b> The Purchase Order is created <b>from the winning Supplier Quotation</b> — rates, terms, and quantities carrying over exactly as quoted, the chain of custody complete: request → RFQ → quotations → comparison → PO, every link a document (the create-from spine's fourth appearance, and its longest chain yet). Awarding by fresh-typed PO at <i>roughly what they quoted</i> breaks the chain at its most valuable link and invites the transcription dispute the chain exists to prevent.</p><p><b>Split awards.</b> Sometimes the right answer is two winners: the volume split between the incumbent and the new second source (the concentration remedy executed — resilience bought at a small blended-price premium, deliberately), or line-level splits where suppliers' strengths differ. The mechanics support it — POs to each from their respective quotations, quantities divided; the decision note carries the reasoning, because a split without a written why reads as indecision to every reviewer after you.</p><p><b>Encoding the winner — closing the loop to the masters.</b> The awarded rate becomes the AGREED rate: a supplier-scoped Item Price on the buying list, validity-dated to the offer's window (Procurement 2, Chapter 3's machinery). This single step is where sourcing's value persists: future routine purchases fetch the competed rate automatically, drift becomes detectable (last-paid vs the encoded rate), and the market test's fruit is stored where every document reads it. The sourcing cycle that skips this step wins the negotiation and then forgets its own victory (Chapter 9's pattern).</p><p><b>Informing the losers.</b> A short, prompt, courteous note to the suppliers who quoted and lost: the ask decided, thank you, we will ask again. Cheap, rare, and disproportionately valuable — the supplier who hears back quotes sharply next time; the one who quoted into silence pads or declines the next ask. Your firm's RFQs have a reputation in the market; this paragraph is where it is set. (What the note never contains: the winner's price, or any competitor's figures — Chapter 6 owns that line and why it is absolute.)</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Negotiation rounds & the ethics line", "13", "<p>The first answers are rarely the last word. This chapter is the legitimate second round — and the bright line that keeps competitive sourcing worth running.</p><p><b>The clarification round.</b> Before any negotiation: questions that make answers comparable — the missing lines quoted, the ambiguous spec confirmed, the delivery basis stated. Clarification goes to whoever needs it, holds the common deadline, and changes no one's commercial position; it is hygiene, not haggling.</p><p><b>The negotiation round.</b> With a comparison in hand, negotiation is legitimate and expected: shortlisted suppliers invited to improve — against a TARGET you state (<i>we need to see this land under ₦X delivered</i>, derived from your comparison and your walk-away arithmetic), or against specific movable dimensions (terms, lead time, MOQ) where the price is fair but the shape is wrong. A disciplined second round is one round: announced as final, closed on schedule, decided. The endless-rounds anti-pattern — reopening whenever any supplier phones with a better number — teaches the market that your deadlines are theatre and your first ask deserves a padded answer.</p><p><b>The bright line: competitors' figures are never shared.</b> Not the winner's price to the losers, not one bidder's number to another mid-round (<i>they came in lower, can you beat it?</i>), not the range, not a hint. The reasons are practical before they are moral: a market that learns your process leaks prices responds by padding first offers (why bid sharp when the real auction starts after?), colluding informally (the leaked numbers become the coordination mechanism), or declining your asks (serious suppliers protect their pricing and avoid buyers who spread it). The leak feels like leverage in the single deal and costs you the sharpness of every future first offer — you are trading the process's integrity for one afternoon's discount. The target-price device gives you the same negotiating force honestly: YOUR number, stated as your requirement, revealing nothing of anyone's bid.</p><p><b>The auction temptation, convicted.</b> The informal descending auction — calling suppliers in turn with ever-lower numbers until someone breaks — is the leak systematised, and it selects for exactly the wrong winner: the supplier desperate enough to break is the one who will recover the margin later through substitution, short-shipment, or collapse mid-contract. Competitive sourcing's purpose is a sustainable best offer, not a broken one.</p><p><b>Validity through the rounds.</b> Each round's improved offers are captured as revised quotations with their own validity — the record showing round one and round two honestly (the amendment discipline of the selling cycle, sourcing edition). Decisions award against the final captured offers; nothing is awarded against a phone number that never became a document.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Sourcing governance: policy, rotation & the file", "13", "<p>Individual sourcing cycles are craft; governance makes the craft a SYSTEM that holds when the buyer is on leave, tempted, or replaced. Four instruments.</p><p><b>The quote policy.</b> Written thresholds: below ₦X, buy at agreed rates or single-quote with a note; above it, three quotes minimum; above ₦Y, formal RFQ with scored comparison and a named approver. Sized to the firm (an 11-person company's thresholds are not a ministry's), written down, and — the part that makes it real — the exceptions are LOGGED as exceptions: the emergency single-source purchase happens (genuine urgency exists), carries its reason, and appears in the quarterly exceptions read. A policy whose exceptions are invisible is a suggestion.</p><p><b>Rotation and the market test.</b> Incumbency is efficient and corrosive in the same motion: the supplier who wins once and is never tested again drifts from market price at the speed of your inattention. The governance: major categories re-quoted on a calendar (annual is a common posture; volatile categories more often), the incumbent competing on equal terms — and told so, which keeps their pricing honest BETWEEN tests. The test is not disloyalty; it is what keeps the relationship priced like a relationship instead of a rent. Where the incumbent wins the test — often, if they are good — the re-award is itself valuable: the rate re-competed, the file refreshed, the loyalty tax audited to zero.</p><p><b>Conflict-of-interest hygiene.</b> The buyer whose relative owns a bidding supplier declares it and steps out of that decision; gifts and hospitality from bidders during live sourcing are declined or disclosed per the firm's line; and the sourcing file's completeness is itself the control — decisions with written reasons, comparisons with declared weights, and awards chained to quotations leave no dark corners for interest to hide in. The system does not assume bad faith; it removes the rooms where bad faith would sit.</p><p><b>The sourcing file.</b> Everything, against the documents: the RFQ and its supplier list, every quotation as captured (originals attached), the normalisation arithmetic, the comparison, the decision note, the award chain, the loser notes, the exceptions log. Not a bureaucratic ornament — the file IS the answers to the questions that will certainly come: the auditor's <i>how was this supplier chosen</i>, the MD's <i>why are we paying this rate</i>, the new buyer's <i>what happened last time</i>, and the dispute's <i>what exactly was quoted</i>. A firm with sourcing files negotiates from institutional memory; a firm without them negotiates from whoever remembers the lunch.</p><p><b>The loop, closed.</b> Governance ends where Procurement 2 began: winner rates encoded as validity-dated, supplier-scoped Item Prices; expiries feeding the re-test calendar; last-paid drift feeding the exceptions eye. Sourcing, masters, and documents — one system, and by now you can see all of its joints.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: sourcing the branded chiller", "16", "<p>Procurement 3 left a thread hanging deliberately: the MD-approved request for a branded counter chiller, routed to sourcing. This chapter runs the full cycle. Every step names its chapter.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the ask (Ch. 1-2).</b> New item, no agreed rate: trigger one, cleanly. The RFQ is created FROM the request (quantities, spec sheet, required-by six weeks — the honest date from Procurement 3's approval), addressed to three suppliers: two Lagos refrigeration vendors from the vetted base, and the Guangzhou manufacturer the ops lead found — who first passes Procurement 1's onboarding gate (verified, documented) before joining the list. Deadline stated: ten working days; validity requested: 30 days; decision timeline shared.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the answers (Ch. 3).</b> Vendor A quotes ₦1.42m delivered, Net-15, 2 weeks lead. Vendor B quotes ₦1.31m EX-WAREHOUSE, advance payment, 3 weeks, and quotes a <i>similar</i> 320L model against the 350L spec — captured as given, flagged, clarified: they confirm the 350L at ₦1.38m. The Guangzhou factory quotes $780 FOB, 45 days, 30% deposit. Three answers, three vocabularies.</p><p><b>Step 3 — normalisation (Ch. 3-4).</b> Common basis, delivered-Lagos, NGN at a stated rate: A stands at ₦1.42m; B becomes ₦1.38m + ₦35k haulage = ₦1.415m; Guangzhou's landed stack (freight share, duty, clearing, haulage at honest FX) lands ≈ ₦1.29m — with the sheet noting the break-even FX and the 45-day lead. Originals filed; arithmetic on the comparison.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the read beyond price (Ch. 4).</b> Landed, Guangzhou is cheapest by ₦125k — but: 45 days against a six-week required-by leaves zero slack (one clearing delay = a missed counter launch); the 30% deposit ties cash for the lead; and a one-unit import carries the whole stack's variance alone. A at ₦1.42m Net-15, two weeks, from a vetted vendor with service presence, prices the risk differently. The weighted read (declared before answers: price 50, lead/risk 30, terms 20) puts A first by a nose.</p><p><b>Step 5 — one honest round (Ch. 6).</b> A target is stated to the two Lagos finalists: <i>land under ₦1.40m delivered and the award closes this week</i>. No competitor figures shared — the target is HealthTrade's number. A returns ₦1.395m, terms held; B holds at ₦1.415m. Revised quotations captured; round closed as announced.</p><p><b>Step 6 — award and the file (Ch. 5, 7).</b> Decision note: A at ₦1.395m — price within target, shortest lead, vetted service presence; Guangzhou noted as the volume play IF chillers become a multi-unit program (the losing quote is market data, filed). PO created from A's revised quotation — request → RFQ → quotes → comparison → PO, chain complete. B and Guangzhou receive prompt courteous notes. The rate is encoded on the buying list, validity-dated, supplier-scoped — and the file closes with every question it will ever be asked already answered.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "13", "<p>The sourcing scar tissue: the tickets and habits, the diseases, the fixes — and the module's law.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — \"The comparison says the import is cheapest\" (and it is not).</b> Disease: raw comparison — factory-price dollars against delivered naira, cartons against units (Ch. 3-4). Fix: the normalisation pass as a mandatory step — common UOM, common currency at a stated rate, common delivery basis, full landed stack — with the arithmetic ON the comparison sheet.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — \"We sourced this last year… nobody remembers how.\"</b> Disease: lunch-sourcing — quotes by phone, decided in a head, filed nowhere (Ch. 1, 7). Fix: the cycle through documents and the sixty-second decision note; the file is the memory that outlives the buyer.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — \"Suppliers' first offers keep getting worse.\"</b> Disease: the market learned your process leaks — shared figures, endless rounds, informal auctions (Ch. 6) — so first offers arrive padded for the real negotiation. Fix: the bright line enforced, one announced final round, targets instead of leaks; the market re-learns within a few cycles that your first ask deserves a sharp answer.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — \"Three quotes on file — two from companies nobody can reach.\"</b> Disease: three-quote theatre — the policy satisfied on paper with solicited-to-lose or fabricated quotes around a pre-decided winner (Ch. 2, 7). Worse than no policy, because it launders the lunch as process. Fix: real candidate lists from the vetted base, exceptions logged AS exceptions when single-sourcing is genuinely right, and the quarterly exceptions read that keeps the difference visible.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — \"We competed it hard, and POs still go out at the old rate.\"</b> Disease: the winner's rate never encoded (Ch. 5) — the victory forgotten by the masters, documents fetching the stale figure. Fix: encoding as a mandatory award step — supplier-scoped, validity-dated — and the last-paid drift eye that catches the miss.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — \"The incumbent has won every year since anyone can remember.\"</b> Disease: eternal incumbency untested (Ch. 7) — possibly deserved, unknowably so, and priced accordingly. Fix: the calendar market test, incumbent competing on equal terms and told so; the loyalty tax audited, whichever way the test lands.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — \"The cheap quote won; the goods are a different product.\"</b> Disease: non-conforming answers silently normalised by assumption (Ch. 3) — the substituted spec became the awarded spec. Fix: capture as given, flag, clarify explicitly; specs confirmed in writing before award; and the receiving gate (Procurement 6, next) as the last line where the substitution is caught with the truck still at the gate.</p><p><b>The law.</b> Procurement 1: purchase documents are downstream of supplier masters. Procurement 2: cost truth begins at the item. Procurement 3: the request layer separates need from commitment. This module adds the fourth: <b>sourcing is a decision record, not a lunch — and competition only works when it is honest and filed.</b> Ask precisely, capture faithfully, compare on a common basis, decide in writing, award through the chain, encode the victory, and never leak a bid. A firm that sources this way is quoted sharply, audited calmly, and remembered accurately by its own files.</p>"),
]

